Transient Synovitis
Undifferentiated or Subclinical Symptom Clusters
Acute non-traumatic unilateral hip pain/limp | Temporal link to recent mild viral illness | Normal plain radiographs with possible mild ultrasound effusion

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: USC-031
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of severe hip pain and refusal to weight-bear, requiring urgent evaluation to exclude septic arthritis.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and parent/caregiver
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an urgent assessment was required to evaluate acute focal joint pain and functional immobility. Immediate clinical examination was mandated to rule out an acute, joint-destroying bacterial infection (septic arthritis) or acute osteomyelitis, preventing irreversible joint destruction prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My leg hurts too much to walk on it, the pain is deep in my hip.
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with an acute onset of hip and knee pain that developed rapidly over the last 12 hours. There is a prominent mild limp and eventual refusal to bear weight on the affected limb. The caregiver notes a preceding mild viral illness (upper respiratory tract infection) five days ago. The patient reports an absence of severe resting pain; the discomfort is primarily movement-dependent. There is a lack of severe local erythema or intense localized warmth. The caregiver confirms the presence of a low-grade or complete absence of fever.
Functional Status: Currently refusing to ambulate unassisted.
Social/Safety Concerns: Caregiver anxiety regarding permanent joint damage.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/70 mmHg | HR: 98 bpm | Temp: 37.3 C | RR: 18 /min | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 35 Kg
General: The patient is in a well-appearing general condition despite localized pain on movement, not exhibiting systemic toxicity. A review of past medical records incidentally notes a history of suppressed TSH with normal free T4/T3 and intermittent subtle palpitations, which is clinically stable and unrelated to the acute orthopedic presentation.
Physical Exam: Musculoskeletal examination of the affected lower limb reveals a mildly limited range of motion, particularly internal rotation of the hip, which elicits guarding. The joint capsule lacks severe localized warmth or fluctuance. There are symmetrical peripheral pulses and normal capillary refill distally. Neurological examination of the lower limbs is intact.
Environmental Observations: Safe, carpeted environment suitable for cautious mobilization.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient took a single dose of paracetamol with minimal relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Transient Synovitis - Status: Acute reactive inflammation.
Differentials:

Septic Arthritis - Ruled out clinically due to the well-appearing nature of the patient, absence of high fever, and lack of severe, intractable resting pain.

Legg-Calve-Perthes Disease - Ruled out as the primary acute cause due to the abrupt onset strictly following a viral prodrome, though requires routine imaging for absolute exclusion.

Slipped Capital Femoral Epiphysis - Ruled out due to demographic incongruence and lack of external rotation posturing, though formal radiography is standard for confirmation.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low.

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient’s sudden refusal to weight-bear and localized hip pain mandated an immediate after-hours home assessment to urgently exclude septic arthritis. Telephone triage appropriately identified the critical risk of rapid cartilaginous destruction inherent in an infected joint. Rigorous physical examination established a classic presentation of post-viral reactive joint inflammation, directly ruling out the toxic, high-fever phenotype of a septic joint. This decisive clinical evaluation successfully prevented an unindicated, highly traumatic emergency department joint aspiration, ensuring the patient remained safe at home under conservative management.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed scheduled weight-based oral NSAIDs for anti-inflammatory control. The expected investigation results include clear synovial fluid without organisms on aspiration and a normal peripheral white blood cell count, which would assist in confirming the diagnosis if symptoms atypically fail to resolve.
Positive Behavioral Change: To reduce mechanical strain on the inflamed joint capsule, maintain strict bed or couch rest and use crutches for any necessary bathroom trips; physically offloading the joint will directly accelerate the reduction of synovial fluid and relieve the pain.
Home-Based Services: None indicated tonight.
DME/Safety: Use of axillary crutches recommended.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 48 hours to confirm improvement and organize an outpatient ultrasound.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the patient develops a high fever, extreme unremitting pain even when completely still, or increased swelling and redness over the joint.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive; acute joint pain in children or young adults must be closely monitored for unexpected progression.
Clinical Course: The expected natural course involves gradual but complete resolution of pain and restoration of full mobility within 7 to 10 days with strict anti-inflammatory use.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency attention if the pain suddenly becomes agonizing while resting, or if the patient develops a high temperature and severe shivering.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: We are using regular, scheduled anti-inflammatories rather than antibiotics because this is a sterile, reactive swelling triggered by a recent cold, not an active bacterial infection.
Actionable Steps: Administer the NSAID strictly with food every eight hours, regardless of pain levels, to maintain a constant suppression of the joint inflammation.

